EN = {
52: "It was then that Akaky Akakievich saw that he could not manage without a new overcoat, and his spirits sank completely. How, indeed, and with what money could it be made? Of course, he could partly rely on the bonus due at the holiday, but that money had long since been allotted and distributed in advance. He needed to get new trousers, to pay the cobbler an old debt for putting new tops on old boot-legs, and he had to order three shirts from a seamstress, and a couple of those items of linen which it is improper to name in print; in a word, all the money had absolutely to be spent, and even if the director were so gracious as to fix, instead of forty roubles' bonus, forty-five or fifty, still there would remain some trifling sum, which in the overcoat fund would be a drop in the ocean.",
53: "Although, of course, he knew that Petrovich was subject to whims of suddenly demanding the devil knows what exorbitant price, so that sometimes even his wife could not help crying out: {W:“What are you doing, have you gone mad, you fool! Another time he will take work for nothing, and now the devil has seized him to ask a price that he is not worth himself.”} Although, of course, he knew that Petrovich would undertake it even for eighty roubles; yet still, where was he to get these eighty roubles? Half he could find: half would turn up; perhaps even a little more; but where to get the other half?.. But first the reader must learn where the first half came from. Akaky Akakievich had the habit, out of every rouble he spent, of putting aside a groat into a small box, locked with a key, with a slit cut in the lid for dropping the money in.",
54: "At the end of every half-year he would go over the copper that had accumulated and exchange it for small silver. He had gone on like this since long ago, and so over the course of several years the sum accumulated proved to be more than forty roubles. So half was in his hands; but where to get the other half? Where to get the other forty roubles?",
55: "Akaky Akakievich thought and thought and decided that he would have to reduce his ordinary expenses, at least for the space of one year: banish the use of tea in the evenings, not light a candle in the evenings, and if there was something he needed to do, go to the landlady's room and work by her candle; walking along the streets, step as lightly and carefully as possible on the stones and flagstones, almost on tiptoe, so as not to wear out the soles too soon; give his linen to the laundress as seldom as possible, and, so that it should not get worn, take it off every time he came home and stay in only a dressing gown of demi-cotton, very old, and spared even by time itself.",
56: "It must be said in truth that at first it was somewhat hard for him to get used to such restrictions, but afterwards somehow he grew used to them and it went well; he even learned quite to go hungry in the evenings; but then he fed spiritually, carrying in his thoughts the eternal idea of the future overcoat. From this time his very existence became somehow fuller, as if he had married, as if some other person were present with him, as if he were not alone, but some pleasant companion of life had agreed to walk the road of life together with him, and this companion was none other than that same overcoat, on thick wadding, on a strong lining that would never wear out. He became somehow livelier, even firmer in character, like a man who has already determined and set himself a goal.",
57: "From his face and his actions doubt disappeared of itself, and hesitation, in a word all wavering and indefinite traits. Fire sometimes showed in his eyes; in his head even the boldest and most daring thoughts flashed: should he not really put marten on the collar? Reflections about this nearly made him absent-minded. Once, while copying a paper, he almost made a mistake, so that he nearly cried out aloud, {A:“Oh!”} and crossed himself. Every month he called on Petrovich at least once to talk about the overcoat, where it was best to buy cloth, and of what colour, and at what price, and though somewhat anxious, he always went home content, reflecting that the time must come at last when all this would be bought and when the overcoat would be made. The matter went even faster than he expected.",
58: "Contrary to all expectation, the director assigned Akaky Akakievich not forty or forty-five, but a whole sixty roubles: whether he had a presentiment that Akaky Akakievich needed an overcoat, or it simply happened so, but in this way he found himself with twenty roubles extra. This circumstance hastened the course of events. Another two or three months of slight hunger, and Akaky Akakievich had collected just about eighty roubles. His heart, generally very calm, began to beat. On the very first day he went with Petrovich to the shops. They bought very good cloth, and no wonder, because they had been thinking about it for half a year already and hardly a month passed without their going into the shops to compare prices; but Petrovich himself said that there was no better cloth to be had.",
59: "For the lining they chose calico, but of such a good, thick sort that, in Petrovich's words, it was even better than silk and even looked handsomer and glossier. They did not buy marten, because it was really dear, and instead chose cat, the best that could be found in the shop, cat which at a distance could always be taken for marten. Petrovich spent altogether two weeks over the overcoat, because there was a great deal of quilting, otherwise it would have been ready sooner. For the work Petrovich took twelve roubles; less was quite impossible: everything was sewn on silk, with a double fine seam, and over every seam Petrovich afterwards went with his own teeth, pressing various patterns into it. It was... hard to say on precisely which day, but probably on the most solemn day in Akaky Akakievich's life, when Petrovich finally brought the overcoat.",
60: "He brought it in the morning, just before the time when one had to go to the department. At no other time would the overcoat have come so opportunely, because rather hard frosts were already beginning and seemed to threaten to grow still stronger. Petrovich appeared with the overcoat as befits a good tailor. In his face there showed an expression so significant as Akaky Akakievich had never yet seen. He seemed to feel fully that he had done no small thing and had suddenly displayed in himself the abyss that divides tailors who merely put in linings and do repairs from those who sew new. He took the overcoat out of the handkerchief in which he had brought it; the handkerchief was fresh from the laundress; he folded it up afterwards and put it in his pocket for use.",
61: "Having taken out the overcoat, he looked very proudly, and, holding it in both hands, threw it very deftly over Akaky Akakievich's shoulders; then he pulled it and smoothed it down at the back with his hand; then he draped it on Akaky Akakievich somewhat loosely open. Akaky Akakievich, as a man of years, wanted to try the sleeves; Petrovich helped him put on the sleeves too, and it turned out that it was good in the sleeves too. In a word, the overcoat proved to fit quite and exactly. Petrovich did not fail on this occasion to say that he had done it so cheaply only because he lived without a signboard in a small street and had known Akaky Akakievich for a long time; on Nevsky Prospect they would have charged seventy-five roubles for the work alone.",
62: "Akaky Akakievich did not want to discuss this with Petrovich, and was afraid of all the large sums with which Petrovich liked to throw dust in people's eyes. He paid him, thanked him and went straight out in the new overcoat to the department. Petrovich went out after him and, remaining in the street, for a long time still looked from afar at the overcoat, and then went purposely aside, so that, going round by a crooked lane, he could run back into the street and look once more at his overcoat from the other side, that is, straight in the face. Meanwhile Akaky Akakievich walked in the most festive disposition of all his senses. He felt every moment of every minute that he had a new overcoat on his shoulders, and several times even smiled with inward pleasure. In truth there were two advantages: one that it was warm, the other that it was handsome.",
63: "He did not notice the road at all and found himself suddenly in the department; in the porter's lodge he took off the overcoat, looked it over all round and entrusted it to the special care of the porter. How it happened is not known, but in the department everybody suddenly learned that Akaky Akakievich had a new overcoat and that the dressing gown no longer existed. Everyone in that same minute ran out into the porter's lodge to look at Akaky Akakievich's new overcoat. They began to congratulate him, to greet him, so that at first he only smiled, and then he even felt ashamed. And when everyone, crowding round him, began to say that the new overcoat must be christened, and that at the very least he must give them all an evening, Akaky Akakievich was completely lost, did not know what to do, what to answer and how to excuse himself.",
64: "A few minutes later, all flushed, he began to assure them, rather simple-heartedly, that it was not a new overcoat at all, that it was nothing, that it was an old overcoat. At last one of the clerks, even some assistant to a head of section, probably in order to show that he was not in the least proud and associated even with those beneath him, said: {C:“So be it, I give the evening instead of Akaky Akakievich and invite you all to tea at my place today: as it happens, it is my name day today.”} The clerks, naturally, at once congratulated the assistant head of section and accepted the invitation willingly. Akaky Akakievich began to decline, but they all began to say that it was discourteous, that it was simply a shame and a disgrace, and he could in no way refuse. Afterwards, however, he felt pleased when he remembered that he would thereby have an occasion to walk out even in the evening in the new overcoat.",
65: "That whole day was for Akaky Akakievich like the greatest solemn holiday. He returned home in the happiest state of mind, took off the overcoat and hung it carefully on the wall, admiring once more the cloth and the lining, and then purposely pulled out, for comparison, his former dressing gown, quite fallen to pieces. He looked at it and even laughed himself: such a distant difference! And for a long time afterwards at dinner he kept smiling whenever the condition of the old garment came into his mind. He dined cheerfully and after dinner wrote nothing, no papers at all, but lounged a little on his bed until it grew dark. Then, not dragging the matter out, he dressed, put the overcoat on his shoulders and went out into the street.",
66: "Where exactly the clerk who had invited him lived, unfortunately, we cannot say: our memory begins to fail us badly, and everything there is in Petersburg, all the streets and houses, have so merged and mixed in our head that it is very hard to get anything out of it in good order. However that may be, at least this is certain, that the clerk lived in the better part of the town, and therefore very far from Akaky Akakievich. At first Akaky Akakievich had to pass through some deserted streets with meagre lighting, but as he approached the clerk's flat the streets became livelier, more populous and brighter lit.",
67: "Pedestrians began to flash by more often, ladies too, handsomely dressed, began to appear, and men with beaver collars; cabmen with their wooden lattice sleighs studded with gilt nails were met more rarely; on the contrary, there were smart drivers in crimson velvet caps, with lacquered sleighs and bear rugs, and carriages with decorated boxes flew along the street, their wheels squealing on the snow. Akaky Akakievich looked at all this as at a novelty. For several years he had not gone out in the evening. He stopped with curiosity before the lighted window of a shop to look at a picture in which some beautiful woman was shown taking off her shoe, thus baring her whole leg, a very shapely one; while behind her back, from the door of another room, a man with whiskers and a handsome little beard under his lip thrust out his head. Akaky Akakievich shook his head and smiled, and then went on his way.",
68: "Why he smiled, whether because he had come across something quite unfamiliar but about which, all the same, everyone retains some sort of instinct, or because he thought, like many other clerks, the following: {A:“Well, those French! What is there to say, if they want something, then it is certainly...”} Or perhaps he did not even think that, for one cannot climb into a man's soul and find out everything he thinks. At last he reached the house where the assistant head of section lived. The assistant head of section lived on a grand scale: a lantern shone on the staircase, the flat was on the second floor. Going into the hall, Akaky Akakievich saw on the floor whole rows of galoshes. Among them, in the middle of the room, stood a samovar, hissing and sending up clouds of steam.",
69: "On the walls hung overcoats and cloaks, some of them even with beaver collars or velvet lapels. Behind the wall noise and talk could be heard, which suddenly became clear and ringing when the door opened and a footman came out with a tray laden with emptied glasses, a cream jug and a basket of rusks. It was plain that the clerks had long since gathered and had drunk their first glass of tea. Akaky Akakievich, having hung up his overcoat himself, went into the room, and before him there flashed at once candles, clerks, pipes, card tables, and his ear was dimly struck by the quick conversation rising from all sides and the noise of chairs being moved. He stopped very awkwardly in the middle of the room, looking about and trying to think what to do. But they had already noticed him, received him with a shout, and all went at once into the hall and again looked over his overcoat.",
70: "Akaky Akakievich, although he was partly abashed, being a sincere man, could not help rejoicing to see everyone praise the overcoat. Then, of course, they all abandoned both him and the overcoat and turned, as usual, to the tables set out for whist. All this, the noise, talk and crowd of people, was somehow wonderful to Akaky Akakievich. He simply did not know what to do, where to put his hands, his feet and his whole figure; at last he sat down beside the players, looked at the cards, peered into one face and another, and after some time began to yawn, to feel bored, the more so since the time at which he usually went to bed had long since come. He wanted to take leave of the host, but they would not let him go, saying that he must by all means drink a glass of champagne in honour of the new garment.",
71: "An hour later supper was served, consisting of vinaigrette salad, cold veal, pâté, pastries and champagne. They made Akaky Akakievich drink two glasses, after which he felt that the room had grown merrier, yet he could not forget that it was already twelve o'clock and that it was long since time to go home. So that the host should not think of detaining him, he went quietly out of the room, found his overcoat in the hall, which he saw, not without regret, lying on the floor, shook it, removed every speck of fluff from it, put it on his shoulders and went down the stairs into the street. In the street it was still light.",
72: "Some little shops, those unchanging clubs of house servants and all sorts of people, were open, and others, which were shut, showed all the same a long stream of light along the whole crack of the door, signifying that they were not yet deprived of company and that probably the maids or servants were still finishing their talk and conversation, plunging their masters into complete bewilderment as to their whereabouts. Akaky Akakievich walked in a merry frame of mind, even ran suddenly, he did not know why, after some lady who passed by like lightning and every part of whose body was full of extraordinary motion. But he stopped at once and went on again as before, very quietly, himself marvelling at the trot that had come from he knew not where. Soon there stretched before him those deserted streets which are not cheerful even by day, and still less in the evening.",
73: "Now they became still more silent and lonely: the street lamps began to flash by more rarely, for less oil was evidently being allowed; wooden houses and fences began; not a soul anywhere; only the snow sparkled in the streets, and the sleeping low hovels with closed shutters showed black and sad. He approached the place where the street was cut across by an endless square, with houses barely visible on its other side, which looked like a fearful desert.",
74: "Far away, God knows where, the light of a fire flickered in some sentry box that seemed to stand at the end of the world. Akaky Akakievich's gaiety here diminished considerably. He stepped onto the square not without some involuntary fear, exactly as if his heart foreboded something evil. He looked back and to the sides: it was exactly like a sea around him. {A:“No, better not to look,”} he thought, and walked on with his eyes shut, and when he opened them to find out whether the end of the square was near, he suddenly saw that before him, almost under his nose, stood some men with moustaches, what sort exactly he could not even make out. His eyes clouded over and his heart pounded.",
75: "{M:“Why, that overcoat is mine!”} said one of them in a voice of thunder, seizing him by the collar. Akaky Akakievich was about to shout “Help!” when the other put a fist the size of a clerk's head right to his mouth, adding: {M:“Just you shout!”} Akaky Akakievich felt only that they took the overcoat off him, gave him a kick with a knee, and he fell on his back in the snow and felt nothing more. After a few minutes he came to himself and got up on his feet, but there was nobody. He felt that it was cold in the open and that the overcoat was gone, began to shout, but his voice, it seemed, had no thought of reaching the ends of the square.",
76: "In despair, not ceasing to shout, he set off running across the square straight to the sentry box, beside which stood a watchman who, leaning on his halberd, was looking, it seems, with curiosity, wishing to know what the devil this man was running towards him from afar and shouting for. Akaky Akakievich, running up to him, began in a breathless voice to shout that he was asleep and was not minding anything, did not see how a man was being robbed. The watchman answered that he had seen nothing, that he had seen two men stop him in the middle of the square, but supposed they were his friends; and that, instead of scolding in vain, he had better go to the inspector tomorrow, and the inspector would find out who had taken the overcoat.",
77: "Akaky Akakievich ran home in complete disorder: his hair, of which a little still grew at the temples and the back of his head, was quite dishevelled; his side and chest and all his trousers were covered with snow. The old woman, his landlady, hearing a terrible knocking at the door, hastily jumped out of bed and ran to open it with a shoe on one foot only, holding her nightgown to her bosom with her hand out of modesty; but on opening she stepped back, seeing Akaky Akakievich in such a state.",
78: "And when he told her what the matter was, she threw up her hands and said that he must go straight to the district superintendent, that the local police officer would cheat him, make promises and lead him a dance; and that best of all was to go straight to the superintendent, that she even knew him, because Anna, the Finnish woman who used to be her cook, was now in service with the superintendent as a nurse; that she often saw him himself driving past their house, and that he also came every Sunday to church, prayed, and at the same time looked cheerfully at everyone, and that therefore, by all signs, he must be a good man. Having heard this decision, Akaky Akakievich plodded sadly to his room, and how he spent the night there is left to be judged by anyone who can in the least imagine another's position.",
79: "Early in the morning he set out for the superintendent; but they said he was asleep; he came at ten, they said again: asleep; he came at eleven o'clock, they said: the superintendent is not at home; he came at dinner time, but the clerks in the anteroom would by no means let him in and wanted to know at all costs on what business and what need had brought him and what had happened. So that at last Akaky Akakievich, once in his life, wanted to show character and said flatly that he must see the superintendent himself in person, that they dared not refuse him admittance, that he had come from his department on official business, and that if he complained of them, then they would see. Against this the clerks dared say nothing, and one of them went to call the superintendent. The superintendent received the story of the robbery of the overcoat in an extremely strange way.",
80: "Instead of turning his attention to the main point of the case, he began to question Akaky Akakievich: why was he returning so late, and had he not called in at, or been to, some disreputable house, so that Akaky Akakievich was quite abashed and left him, not knowing himself whether the case of the overcoat would take its proper course or not. All that day he was not at the office (the only occasion in his life). The next day he appeared quite pale and in his old dressing gown, which had become still more pitiful. The tale of the robbery of the overcoat, in spite of the fact that there were clerks who did not let even this pass without laughing at Akaky Akakievich, nevertheless touched many.",
81: "They decided on the spot to make a collection for him, but collected the merest trifle, because the clerks had already spent a great deal, subscribing to the director's portrait and to some book, at the suggestion of the head of the department, who was a friend of the author; so the sum turned out the merest trifle. Somebody, moved by compassion, resolved at least to help Akaky Akakievich with good advice, telling him not to go to the local police officer, because although it might happen that the officer, wishing to earn the approval of his superiors, would somehow find the overcoat, the overcoat would nevertheless remain at the police unless he produced legal proof that it belonged to him; but that best of all was to apply to a certain Important Person, that the Important Person, by writing and corresponding with whom it was proper, could make the matter go forward more successfully. There was nothing to be done: Akaky Akakievich resolved to go to the Important Person.",
}
